# Reviewed gitleaks findings that are not credentials, one EXACT fingerprint each.
#
# WHY FINGERPRINTS AND NOT PATHS. A fingerprint is commit:file:rule:line, so an entry silences the
# one finding a person read and nothing else: not the next line of the same file, not the same file
# in a later commit. A path entry in `.gitleaks.toml` would silence every future secret in that
# file, which is why that file allowlists fixture paths only and real source ends up here.
#
# HOW TO ADD ONE. Read the finding (the CI log prints it redacted, with its fingerprint), confirm it
# is not a credential, then add the fingerprint with a line saying what the string actually is. If
# it IS a credential, rotate it first: this file stops the report, not the exposure.
#
# DESCRIBE THE VALUE, DO NOT QUOTE IT. This file is scanned like any other, and a fake pasted into a
# comment here is a new finding in a file no allowlist covers.

# cli/internal/creds/creds.go — `passwordAlphabet`, the character SET generated passwords are drawn
# from (a-z, A-Z, 2-9 minus the look-alikes). It is the alphabet, not a password.
